WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
 So again, I think Paul really created for us the first search engine and showed that the web could be a mostly consumed environment and be extremely useful.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:17.000
 When you think about it, if there was no web and there was no content, how would you know that the web is a great way to view content?

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:26.000
 So Paul Coons had the advantage that he had a lot of content, and it was content that at least the physicists found extremely valuable, and then people go, "Oh, I can see."

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.000
 As a matter of fact, I remember when I first saw the web, I was like, "Okay, fine. It's got pictures. Who cares?"

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.000
 Because other things like Gopher were just as good.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
 And then I saw the ability to go to Federal Express and track a package, and I go, "Now that's a cool idea."

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.000
 Gopher couldn't do that.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:52.000
 As a matter of fact, in 1993, sort of three years after the web was created, the web was actually not all that popular.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:59.000
 1991 was the Paul Coons, but that was mostly physicists and a few nerds, myself included.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000
 But in 1993, Gopher was a much better product, and it was a much more beloved product.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:14.000
 And the problem, again, came down to the fact that the network was so slow that simple text-based things worked better than highly graphical things.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:24.000
 There's an apocryphal story that happened in March of 1993 where there was a meeting of the Internet Engineering Task Force that does standards for all these things.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
 And they had a bird-of-a-feather session for Gopher and a bird-of-a-feather session for the World Wide Web.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
 And the Gopher session was full of people, and they couldn't have enough in the room, and they were sitting on the floor and peeking in the door.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
 And then later, I wasn't at this meeting, by the way.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
 Rich Wiggins, my co-host on the television show, was at this meeting.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
 And what he tells me is, at the bird-of-a-feather meeting, there was almost nobody there.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:56.000
 And Turnburner's Lee was like, "I've been working on this thing for three years, and it's better than Gopher, but nobody wants to use it."

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.000
 And the people in the room at that point told him, they said, "It's just too complex. It's too hard to get working."

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:11.000
 So this was a long time ago, and the Web was not assured to be a success at that point. It just wasn't really clear.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:17.000
 The university was getting the idea that we had to spend a lot of money on a campus-wide network

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.000
 and that maybe we should use it for something besides connecting to the mainframe.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.000
 So most of the top of the computer center was saying maybe we should do something with publishing information.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:34.000
 That'd be an interesting sort of use of the network that more people could use than just the people who wanted to talk to the mainframe.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
 Were you surprised at how fast Gopher took off?

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:43.000
 Yeah, absolutely shocked, because we basically wrote something that we thought would be good enough for our campus

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:49.000
 and hoped that a few other places would use it because then if somebody at a different institution used it,

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.000
 we'd have some credibility locally that, "Hey, we're not just saying it's good. Somebody else is saying it's good."

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
 So that it was accepted is probably more a sign that it just happened to be the right thing at the right time.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.000
 The environment was ripe for something like that, and we had an okay product at the right time.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.000
 Basically, we put it up for anonymous FTP and sent out a couple announcements on Newsnet saying,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.000
 "Hey, here's a thing that does kind of a campus-wide information system."

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:21.000
 The fashion back then was to say, "Everybody needs to have a CWIS, a campus-wide information system."

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:26.000
 So Gopher, we thought, made a pretty good one because he didn't have to centralize publishing on one machine.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:32.000
 You could let individual departments publish off their own machines and control their own content.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.000
 That model turned out to be a pretty good one, so lots of other places started using it.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:43.000
 So we had kind of the right idea at the point where this particular issue, campus-wide information system,

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:49.000
 was something that all of the university computer centers were saying, "I've got to have one of these."

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:57.000
 The technology we built Gopher to work with were fairly slow machines with limited graphics and slow modems.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.000
 That was our design point because that's what people had six, seven years ago.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.000
 And while we added graphics and movies and those sorts of things,

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.000
 Gopher wasn't designed to be hypertext and really media-rich documents.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.000
 It was aimed more at run fast over limited bandwidth on slow machines.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:24.000
 So 1993 was kind of like, it was a real, there was a lot of things that could have been very different in 1993.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:31.000
 And I want to show you a commercial, and there's a couple of these out there,

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:38.000
 from 1993 that one of the large telecommunications vendors put out as a national ad campaign.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.000
 And this is what the telecommunications industry was thinking about.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.000
 They saw it all happening. They saw this all happening. They knew it was going to be big.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.000
 They knew it was going to allow interactions in many ways. They knew it. They weren't dumb.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.000
 It was like, "Whoa, what's going on?" No, not at all. They absolutely knew.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:05.000
 So this really wonderful series of commercials is just quite amazing.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:11.000
 So take a look. At the end you'll see actually what the large telecommunications company's name is.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.000
 So take a quick look.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:06:05.000
 So again, sort of looking back at this and looking at things that might not have been,

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:14.000
 there's nobody that really makes the connection that Steve Jobs might have had some impact on the World Wide Web.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.000
 But in a way, he was actually quite influential.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:25.000
 Steve Jobs, of course, founded Apple and then was kicked out of Apple after the Macintosh.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.000
 And he started a new company called Next.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.000
 And if you listen closely to what Robert Caillou says, and you listen closely to what Paul Kuhn says,

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:38.000
 and the computer that was on my desk during this entire period of the 1990s to '93 were Next computers.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:45.000
 Next was a bold, Unix-based, highly networked, high-definition display, high-performance computer

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:50.000
 that Steve Jobs built when he formed the company Next after he got fired from Apple.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.000
 Of course, he eventually came back to Apple, and the Next technology is Macintosh.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.000
 That's the Macintosh operating system.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:03.000
 And so if you make a mistake in Macintosh, you might see an error message that starts "NS something."

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:08.000
 That's called NextStep, which is the operating system on these Next computers.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.000
 For the first three years of the Web, it was pretty much only on the Next computer.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:18.000
 People would, even the server was on the Next computer, and the browser was on the Next computer.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:21.000
 And so the Next computer really kind of did that.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:31.000
 I wrote an article a couple months after Steve Jobs died for IEEE Computer Magazine's January 2012 issue

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:35.000
 that really sort of tried to, at least from a historical perspective,

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:41.000
 point out how important Steve Jobs may have been in helping the Internet get formed.

